\chapter{Acknowledgements}
There is a certain level of selfishness inherent in working on a dissertation.
It's a project that takes an enormous amount of time and effort that in turn are not available for other tasks, and while the end product helps add to humankind's knowledge creation venture, its most immediate reward is experienced entirely by the researcher in the form of a bestowed title and a sense of satisfaction.
Despite this, a dissertation cannot be completed in isolation, which makes all the contributions of all those in the orbit of the researcher along the way just as selfless as the dissertation project is selfish.
It's with this in mind that I must thank all those who have helped me along the way.

To begin with, there would not have been an object of study available if it were not for the world that the people of Louisiana have built.
Inspired to learn more about my own family history in the state about which I had only been taught very little, I first visited Louisiana when I moved to New Orleans in 2012 to complete my bachelor's degree.
Almost immediately after arriving, Hurricane Isaac hit.
I found myself lacking resources of every kind, but as I struggled through the aftermath with the strangers around me, I found an altruistic goodness in them at every turn and a refusal to let struggle stop them from making the most of life.
I instantly fell in love with the people, their way of life, as well as the landscape.

Lafayette and the surrounding parishes were not my first stop in Louisiana, but the community I found there had that same vibrancy that I found in New Orleans.
It was therefore no surprise that I was able to find so many people who wanted to help me as I searched for participants for this study, whether that meant becoming participants themselves, helping me find participants, or just being good to me.
Of course, I am especially thankful to the Creoles and Cajuns who did lend their time and voices to this study by allowing me to interview them.
I walked away from every interview feeling like I had just been let in on a few more secrets about this place and these people.
Every interaction was not just enlightening but rather uplifting, and I cannot express enough appreciation for that.

Knowing what to do with this enlightenment required the help of the linguistics department at the University of Georgia.
I must thank not only my major professor, Jon Forrest, and my dissertation committee members, Diana Ranson and Joshua Bousquette, for their wisdom and guidance but also everyone at the university who was involved in the academic growth I experienced there.
This was truly a supportive, encouraging program that provided me with a wealth of opportunities.

Last but not least are my family and friends who were there for me outside of my studies.
From my parents who, one way or another, endowed me with the endless curiosity to take my education this far and who made sure that I have always had a safety net underneath me to my parents-in-law who have equally encouraged and supported me to the rest of my family in New Jersey and Chicago who have always been there for me and have even expressed their own curiosity about my work, which has pushed me to learn how to explain it and its value to non-specialists.
That may sound like a jest, but sharing knowledge has always felt just as important to me as generating it.

My friends have also often shown that support and curiosity about my work, but they have also been a reminder that it's important to take breaks from it.
Whether it has been a night out, a holiday party, or just an opportunity to talk about something other than language, those moments have been incredibly helpful for finding more balance in my life and ultimately being better at both life and work as a result.

The two most important people in my world, though, and the two people I could never have done this without, are my wife, Shari Ex, and my son, Otto McNeill, who joined our world in the middle of my doctoral program.
In addition to Shari's endless support, they are responsible for so much joy in my life that I cannot fathom doing anything without them let alone accomplishing a task as large as the present one.
They inspire me on a daily basis and always remind me of what is most important.
Shari, thank you so much.
I love you.
And Otto, merci d'être toi.
Je t'aime gros pour toujours.